<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Laguna de Términos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Laguna de Términos is a large coastal lagoon in the Mexican state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campeche">Campeche</a>, separated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> by the island of Carmen. It was named by Spanish explorers in 1518. Rivers including the Candelaria flow into it, and in Maya times it was a hub of the canoe trade on the routes around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán Peninsula</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/laguna-de-terminos.jpg" alt="Laguna de Términos" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA Earth Observatory images by Joshua Stevens, using Landsat data from the U.S. Geological Survey. Story by Kathryn Hansen. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laguna_de_T%C3%A9rminos.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Its name, Lagoon of Ends, comes from the first Spanish explorers, who thought it marked the end of the island of Yucatán; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-grijalva">Juan de Grijalva</a> came upon the Boca de Términos on his expedition of 1518. The lagoon is 72 km long and 24 km wide and covers 1,550 km2, the largest tidal lagoon by volume lying wholly on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>. Two channels link it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-campeche">Bay of Campeche</a>, the Boca de Puerto Real and the Boca del Carmen, and the tides renew about half its water every nine days.</p><p>Besides the Candelaria, the Chumpan and two distributaries of the Usumacinta, the Palizada and the San Pedro y San Pablo, feed it through smaller lagoons; the Palizada brings about 75% of its fresh water, so the western end is richer in nutrients and less salty. Mangroves line its swampy shores, some over 30 metres tall near the Palizada's outlet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Several Late Postclassic Maya sites lie on the surrounding barrier islands, beach ridges and mangrove coast. The region was home to Xicallanco, or Xicalango, perhaps on the western shore, linked in name to the Olmeca-Xicallanca people who came after the twelfth century. Xicalango was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> inhabited mainly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahuas</a> and an important port of trade between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> and the Maya; by the 16th century Aztec pochteca, long-distance merchants interested above all in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a>, sat on its governing council.</p><p>In 1525 Xicalango destroyed the supply ships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> and killed their crews. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo">Francisco de Montejo</a> conquered it in 1529, but his hold weakened after he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campeche">Campeche</a> in 1531 and his son had to reclaim it in 1536. By 1550 it was largely abandoned and its remaining people were moved to Jonuta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The city of Ciudad del Carmen stands on Isla del Carmen, and the Zacatal Bridge of Federal Highway 180 joins the island to the mainland. The Mexican government declared the lagoon a protected area for flora and fauna in 1994; 1,468 animal species have been identified there, and the state oil company <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pemex">Pemex</a> operates within it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Laguna de Términos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Acalan, meaning 'place of canoes' in Nahuatl, was the land of the Chontal Maya (Mactun) along the Candelaria river south of the <b>Laguna de Términos</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/acalan">Acalan</a></li></ul></section>
